# GARNS Declarativity Baseline

This is a bounded source audit for the seven exploration areas, not a full runtime
correctness review. The inspected implementation is the working tree based on
commit 715b54e. Unrelated in-progress worker and PostgreSQL contract edits are not
treated as accepted language behavior.

## Existing strengths

GARNS already separates declarations, qualified semantic IR and authored physical
bindings. Intents centralize field meaning; references are typed; traits compose
fields; queries describe results; live questions have an intentionally closed,
bounded algebra. Evolution already distinguishes logical continuity from physical
renaming. These are valuable foundations, not scaffolding to discard automatically.

## Evidence and gaps

| Area | Observed baseline | Classification and source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Relationships | Link target, deletion policy, optionality and combined key flags; explicit physical mapping | Implemented baseline; [grammar](../../grammar/garns.lark), [SQLite lowering](../../src/garns/lower_sqlite.py) |
| Constraints | Closed sets enforced; carrier invariants resolve; intent pattern/length are carried but unconsumed | Mixed implementation; [reference](../../docs/DSL_REFERENCE.md), [limitations](../../docs/LIMITATIONS.md) |
| Composition | Trait uses/links expand; conflicts and cycles checked | Implemented baseline; [resolver](../../src/garns/resolve.py) |
| Trait invariants | Invariant retained on trait but absent from consuming resource | Reproduced resolver defect below, not a language feature request |
| Cross-object invariants | Runtime checks the written carrier row after mint/change, not all affected dependent owners | Source inspection; [engine](../../src/garns/engine.py), method `_check_invariants`; no concurrent runtime proof here |
| World/targets | World includes semantic policies and generation targets; deployment selects engine and location | Existing grammar, mixed responsibility rather than absence |
| Values | Scalar intents, nominal newtypes, restricted type constructors; Opaque presence-only | Deliberate current restrictions, not a recursive document contract; [types](../../src/garns/types.py) |
| Modules | Named imports and aliases, qualified identity, acyclic import graph; no parameters | Implemented baseline plus genuine new design area; [reference](../../docs/DSL_REFERENCE.md) |
| Operations | Restricted/bulk/compound/alias IR validated; generated code iterates reads only | Explicitly unimplemented execution, not merely missing application declarations; [limitations](../../docs/LIMITATIONS.md) |
| Async/PostgreSQL | Accepted architecture and pure contracts are not production adapter proof | [W1 acceptance](../W1-ACCEPTANCE.md), [A2](../../docs/adr/A2-backend-contract.md) |

Paths in the final row are relative to this file: W1 acceptance is in the parent
developer-docs directory; ADRs live under the repository docs directory.

The current backend iteration deliberately preserved grammar and deferred full verb
execution and dormant declaration consumers. See
[the implementation plan](../GarnsV9-6-PostgresAsyncImplementationPlan.md) sections
on out-of-scope work and declaration completion. These are sequencing decisions,
not evidence that reusable constraints or operations are undesirable.

## Reproduced constraint propagation defect

Executed once with the existing parser/resolver, using an in-memory source string;
no database or source files were changed. The following is current GARNS syntax:

```garns
module trial "trait propagation probe" {
  intent lower : Integer "lower bound"
  intent upper : Integer "upper bound"
  trait Bounds "two bounds" {
    use lower
    use upper
    invariant lower <= upper
  }
  resource Window "bounded window" { carry Bounds }
}
world TEST "probe" {
  modules trial
  durability durable
  writers governed
  generated python
  scope deployment
  quarantine_retention 3
}
```

Observed resolved IR:

```text
trial.Bounds uses=2 invariants=1
trial.Window uses=2 invariants=0
```

`Resolver.resolve_carrier` copies uses/links; pending invariants contain only locally
declared predicates. `resolve_reads_and_invariants` does not add carried predicates.
The probe establishes missing IR propagation, not a measured database corruption.
All four bundle alternatives require this class of defect to be impossible or caught.

## Declarativity priorities

1. Make declarations trustworthy: consume enforced-looking constraints or refuse
   their unsupported use before effects. Adding syntax without consumers worsens this.
2. Let intent and composition rules retain their identity and invariants everywhere.
3. Specify allowed changes and atomic guards as data, not handwritten application checks.
4. Distinguish presence and values before choosing storage encodings.
5. Make generation/deployment choices visible without redefining domain meaning.
6. Preserve explainability as module specialization and derived effects grow.

Only the propagation probe was executed. No new runtime, generic-module compiler,
native database adapter, full suite or external language comparison was tested.
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/DocumentValues.md
# Document Value Alternatives

Required meaning: finite recursive null, Boolean, exact number, Unicode text,
ordered sequence and unique-text-key mapping. Omission differs from explicit null;
false and zero are values. Mapping order and numerical spelling do not change
meaning; sequence order does. Unknown fields survive edits. Original bytes are
separate observations, not the canonical value. No option equates this with JSONB.

The word intent below means a GARNS declaration; an evaluation scenario is called
a use case to avoid confusing the rubric's separate use of that word.

## V1 One exact document scalar with typed access operators

```text
intent content: DocumentValue
query member_presence { show content.lookup("note") }  # Missing | Present(Value)
operation edit { input patch: DocumentPatch }
```

Add a closed recursive scalar to the value universe plus a small typed lookup/update
algebra. `lookup` returns a presence sum; Present(Null) is not Missing. Patch entries
are Keep, Remove or Set(value); an omitted patch entry means Keep. ByteBlob stores
observations. Exact decimal normalization is versioned and never uses binary float
as an intermediate.

Valid: set note to Null while leaving another unknown member unchanged. Refusal:
duplicate object keys, nonfinite numbers, malformed Unicode or a value beyond declared
bounds. Format validation remains a separate pure contract, not the carrier schema.

Cost medium/high; codecs, operators, limits and canonicalization are real work, but
carrier IR changes remain relatively small. Strong fit with incremental A. Weakness:
member types are not statically known without explicit narrowing; generic queries
can devolve into many guarded path operations. Adapters may use blobs or native
documents only if all advertised predicates and exactness semantics are preserved.

## V2 Structural value schemas with open extensions

```text
value Subject {
  name: Text required
  note: nullable Text optional
  extras: open DocumentValue
}
intent content: map<Text, Subject>
```

Declare known shapes while retaining unknown members in a lossless extension map.
Presence and nullability are independent. A schema field is absent, present-null
if permitted, or present-value. A structural value is not B2's flat intent bundle:
it is a nested value with defined equality and projection semantics.

Valid: typed query of subject.name and round-trip preservation of an unfamiliar
member. Refusal: extras collides with a declared name, a wrong typed field is hidden
in extras, or removal of a required field. Open extras have no implicit edit authority;
Pyrocat operations preserve them unless their format explicitly allows modification.

Cost high; recursive shape IR, generated types and migration of shape refinements.
Pairs well with C2 refined types and M2 signatures. It removes repetitive narrowing
but requires a declared schema for detailed research. Exact numbers and canonical
identity still need the same primitives as V1. Evolving an unknown field into a
known one validates existing values instead of silently coercing them.

## V3 Versioned format contracts and typed projections

```text
format CatalogDocument version 1 {
  value DocumentValue
  projection subjects: sequence<Subject>
  preserves unknown_members
  canonicalization ExactDocumentV1
}
intent content: CatalogDocument
```

Keep the recursive value and give it a named interpretation contract. One declarative
projection description derives typed research surfaces; an external format adapter
may implement bounded codecs but cannot mutate catalog state. The raw value remains
authoritative. Projections are derived, versioned and not independently editable.

Valid: a subject query follows the interpretation version pinned on its revision.
Refusal: adapter returns a projection inconsistent with its source fingerprint,
changes interpretation under the same version, or cannot preserve unknown fields.
Projection reconciliation must run under acceptance atomicity if materialized.

Cost high; explicit format boundary and independent codec/projection evidence needed.
Fits C3/M3 and state-transition operations. Without a defined projection algebra this
would merely hide imperative interpretation behind a name; that version fails the
meaningful-derivation gate. The viable option includes inspected projection expressions
and a separately identified, hermetic codec boundary. Complex formats remain a risk.

## V4 Explicit recursive value model

```text
value Value = Null | Bool(Boolean) | Number(ExactNumber) | Text(Text)
            | Sequence<ordered Value> | Object<unique Text => Value>
intent content: Value
projection SubjectNames from Object entries ...
```

Represent the document algebra explicitly in the semantic model, with generated
visitor, construction and projection APIs. Values form trees; physical sharing is
an adapter optimization, not observable identity. Sequences carry order; objects
carry unique keys; a missing entry has no node. Original bytes remain separate.

Valid: traverse or transform any node without loss of unfamiliar structure. Refusal:
cycles, two entries with the same key, unordered sequence data, or accidental use of
storage node identity as value equality. A patch still distinguishes Keep/Remove/Set.

Cost very high for ergonomic bulk APIs and bounded traversal; particularly costly
if lowered naively into many rows. Fits relational/rule-oriented D without making
SQL tables the model. Exact reconstruction and efficient large-value operations
must be demonstrated. It offers maximal inspectability at the cost of exposing more
structural machinery to authors; generated conveniences cannot change semantics.

## Shared boundaries and comparison

Define exact number domain, canonical algorithm, negative-zero treatment, Unicode
policy and depth/size limits before implementation. Proposed meaning equates decimal
spellings of the same finite mathematical value, including signed zero; source bytes
preserve spelling. No Unicode normalization should be silently introduced. Concrete
resource limits are capability profiles, not reasons to truncate values.

| Option | Gates and derivation | Best property | Concern | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| V1 | P/P/P; typed document operators and codecs | Small domain surface | Runtime path narrowing | Medium/high |
| V2 | P/P/P; recursive shape validation/projections | Typed known fields plus retained unknowns | Structural-type machinery | High |
| V3 | P/P/P; declared format projections | Versioned research interpretations | Escape-boundary fidelity | High |
| V4 | P/P/P; algebra-generated visitors/projections | Explicit complete value model | Authoring and storage expansion | Very high |

The current Opaque type is not any of these contracts. All four preserve the same
test vectors: omitted/null/false/zero, decimal spellings, ordered arrays, unknown
fields, source formatting and oversized input refusal. Large-input qualification
must include the roughly 100 MB inventory; no performance claim is made here.
See [baseline](Baseline.md) and [type implementation](../../src/garns/types.py).
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/IntentBundles.md
# Intent Bundle Alternatives

The requested bundle is macro-like: companion fields and their laws appear at every
use. It is not a nested object or a collection. All alternatives require typed,
qualified expansion with source provenance, not textual substitution.

## B1 Complete trait and carry semantics

```text
trait Bounds { use lower use upper invariant lower <= upper }
resource Window { carry Bounds }
```

Retain the existing construct, repair invariant propagation and make trait expansion
the canonical reuse mechanism. For repeated use, add an explicit mapping such as
`carry Bounds rename {lower => start, upper => finish}`. The mapping introduces
member roles while preserving the originating intent identity; these two concepts
must not be conflated by an unqualified-name API.

Valid: Window inherits both fields and the predicate. Refusal: two mapped members
claim the same role with incompatible policy. Existing narrowing/conflict checks
extend to predicates and keys. No override can remove an inherited guarantee.

Cost medium; repeated uses need more than today's intent-keyed field model. Existing
single-use schemas remain closest to current GARNS. Traits are broad enough to include
links, which is helpful but can blur a value bundle with a resource role. Fits C1/M1;
an imported trait must bind its original intents, not local lookalikes. Expansion
origin maps support change impact and migration. Current missing invariants are a
defect to fix regardless of whether this becomes the chosen language design.

## B2 Explicit typed expansion bundles

```text
bundle Bounds {
  component lower: Integer
  component upper: Integer
  invariant lower <= upper
}
resource Window {
  use Bounds as opening
  use Bounds as closing
}
```

Bundle components become flat logical carrier members identified by bundle instance
and component. `opening::lower` is a role qualifier, not evidence of nested storage.
Each expansion gets the substituted invariant and component defaults/refinements.
No separate bundle object is allocated at runtime.

Valid: opening and closing bounds coexist with independent constraints. Refusal:
renaming opening::lower to a role already occupied by another declaration, recursive
bundle expansion, or dropping one required companion. Optional whole bundles need
an all-absent/all-present rule; optional components remain a separate contract.

Cost medium/high; a dedicated IR expansion node plus canonical flattening is required.
Better role identity than ad-hoc field renaming; introduces another declaration kind
unless traits are retired or precisely scoped to reference-bearing roles. Fits C2/M2,
also C1/M4. Evolution compares component identity and instance mappings so a shared
edit yields a complete affected-carrier report, not silent regenerated names.

## B3 Signature-governed module fragments

```text
module Bounds exports BundleShape {
  intent lower: Integer
  intent upper: Integer
  rule ordered: lower <= upper
}
resource Window { expand Bounds as opening }
```

Use one composition system: modules can export a closed bundle-shaped fragment.
An explicit signature distinguishes expandable members/rules from ordinary resources,
operations and private dependencies. Importing a module does not automatically expand
it; `expand` selects the declared fragment.

Valid: a parameterized bounds module supplies its exported fragment. Refusal: selecting
only lower while the selected bundle contract requires upper and ordered; selecting
an operation as a field fragment. Transitive private dependencies are retained without
being exposed as author-visible members.

Cost high initially, potentially lower long-term if generic modules are already
required. Strong fit with M2/M3 and C3; relatively heavyweight without signatures.
Canonical identity uses the module instance, export and expansion site. Evolution
must distinguish changed private implementation from changed exported meaning.
Concern: coupling simple field reuse to sophisticated module checking and instantiation.

## B4 Companion implication rules

```text
companion_rule Bounds {
  having lower in a carrier requires upper in that carrier
  invariant lower <= upper
}
resource Window { use lower with Bounds }
```

The author opts into a named companion rule set. Compiler computes a finite monotone
closure of required declarations, then attaches predicates. The rule names are explicit;
there is no ambient global rule that changes every future use of lower silently.
Only declaration addition is admitted, not overwriting, deletion or runtime effects.

Valid: lower brings upper and the relationship rule. Refusal: contradictory required
defaults, name collision, or recursive parameter expansion that generates unbounded
new roles. A finite cycle over the same declarations can terminate at a fixed point;
the compiler must prove that finite universe or refuse it.

Cost high; meaningful automatic closure reduces repetition but makes consequences
less obvious. Fits C4/R4 when they share a rule engine; poor fit with opaque inherited
overrides. Require an expansion explanation showing each introduced field and why.
Versioned rule-set membership and closure diffs are essential migration artifacts.

## Comparison

| Option | Gates and derivation | Primary advantage | Main risk | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| B1 | P/P/P; trait expansion | Reuses existing abstraction | Role renaming extends current identity model | Medium |
| B2 | P/P/P; hygienic component expansion | Clear value-bundle semantics | Another construct unless traits are consolidated | Medium/high |
| B3 | P/P/P; signature-checked export expansion | One generic composition mechanism | Module complexity for small bundles | High |
| B4 | P/P/P; finite companion closure | Says only the initiating declaration | Nonlocal consequences and closure proofs | High |

The invariant-propagation probe in [Baseline.md](Baseline.md) is the only executed
evidence here. All four new semantics are unimplemented. Common tests must include
two instances, diamond composition, predicate rebinding, conflicting keys/defaults,
optional-group presence and a migration affecting several consuming carriers.
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/MutationContracts.md
# Declarative Mutation Alternatives

Requirement: state permitted changes once, including inputs, trusted authority,
expected state, effects and returns. Acceptance of a proposal must create a consistent
revision/evidence/decision or nothing. Scope and field authorization differ from an
input allowlist. Queries/questions remain read-only.

The four alternatives share the laws in [operation examples](examples/OperationLaws.md).
They differ in what the author describes: steps, effects, transitions or desired state.
All are static descriptions before execution, not embedded Python procedures.

## O1 Bounded declarative compounds

```text
compound Accept(p: Proposal, expected: optional Revision) atomic {
  authority accept(p.document)
  require pending(p) and current(p.document) is expected
  require validated_exactly(p)
  step r = Revision.mint(from p) when changed_identity(p)
  step d = Decision.mint(result = r otherwise current(p.document))
  returns d
}
```

Extend existing compounds with typed parameters, guards, conditional steps, returns
and authority. Step dependencies must be explicit; bounded, inspectable sequence
order can remain local. Compiler derives input validation, failure propagation,
reference binding, write effects and one transaction plan.

Valid: a decision depends on the selected revision result. Refusal: unbound earlier
step, conflicting writes, guard outside transaction or an effectful callback hidden
in a condition. An arbitrary loop or host-language step is not admitted.

Cost high despite familiar grammar: current verbs have no runtime. Incremental reuse
is strongest, generativity lowest because authors still wire most mutation steps.
Fits M1/B1/V1. Good explainability through a plan trace. Evolution preserves named
operation identity while versioning its complete intent and guards for replay.

## O2 Atomic effect contracts

```text
operation Accept(p: Proposal, expected: optional Revision) {
  authority accept(p.document)
  require pending(p) and current(p.document) is expected and validated_exactly(p)
  effects atomic {
    r = accepted_revision(p)  # new if full identity differs, otherwise existing
    create Decision(proposal=p, result=r, disposition=disposition_for(r))
  }
  returns Decision
}
```

Declare a finite set of typed create/update/delete/assert effects, with data references
that induce dependencies. The author states resulting facts rather than every ordering
step. `accepted_revision` above is a declared reusable effect fragment, not a magic
runtime builtin; its full definition appears in the common examples.

Valid: compiler orders revision creation before referencing decision and includes
evidence closure. Refusal: two effects assign incompatible final values, an unresolved
effect dependency, or a cyclic creation pattern unsupported by the target's admitted
identity-allocation strategy. Do not invent a solver to choose an arbitrary result.

Cost high; requires a small effect IR and complete affected-state validation. Fits
C2/B2/M2/V2; adapters choose execution details under the same atomic guarantee.
Less imperative than O1 without the general inference burden of O4. Diagnostics
must explain derived dependencies, protected read sets and original author effects.

## O3 State transition contracts

```text
transition Accept(p: Proposal) from Pending to Decided {
  input expected: optional Revision
  authority accept(p.document)
  guard current(p.document) is expected and validated_exactly(p)
  establish Accepted(p, accepted_revision(p)) or Equivalent(p, current(p.document))
  returns outcome
}
```

Model legal state changes and their object facts explicitly. Pending/Decided can be
derived from absence/presence of Decision, not a second mutable status field. Alternative
outcomes have disjoint exhaustive guards; they are not nondeterministic choices.
The compiler derives transition admission and disallows operations on immutable
facts outside their declared creation/removal rules.

Valid: Accept and Discard compete for the same Pending state. Refusal: overlap between
accepted and equivalent guards, a missing outcome branch, or an unconstrained transition
that rewrites historical revisions. General guard disjointness may be undecidable;
require a closed discriminator or explicit case partition rather than guessing.

Cost high/very high. Excellent for workflow laws, awkward for ordinary bulk updates
unless those can use reusable transitions without artificial states. Fits M3/C3/V3.
Evolution includes transition compatibility for pending objects and pinned operations;
state names changing do not justify rewriting old decisions.

## O4 Bounded desired-state reconciliation

```text
mutation Accept(p: Proposal, expected: optional Revision) {
  authority accept(p.document)
  require pending(p) and current(p.document) is expected and validated_exactly(p)
  desired within p.document {
    accepted_meaning = p.meaning_with_evidence
    terminal_decision(p) = accepted_or_equivalent
  }
  preserve all_except declared_targets
  returns terminal_decision(p)
}
```

Declare a desired model fragment and preservation boundaries. A deterministic planner
derives the minimal admitted change set using explicit identity/matching rules. It is
one bounded transaction reconciliation, not an autonomous background loop. Desired
state never authorizes deletion merely because data was omitted.

Valid: an already-equivalent meaning adds a decision without duplicating a revision.
Refusal: ambiguous identity match, conflicting desired facts, effects outside the
declared region, or a plan that rewrites immutable evidence. Matching rules must yield
one result; an underspecified optimization problem refuses.

Cost very high. Highest potential engine derivation, largest explainability and
correctness burden. Fits V4/R2/M4 if the value and identity model is explicit. Evolving
matching/default rules changes operation semantics and must be versioned. A dry-run
is useful explanation, but actual guards must still be rechecked atomically at commit.

## Comparison and required proofs

| Option | Gates and derivation | Benefit | Concern | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| O1 | P/P/P; guarded transactional step planning | Builds on existing verbs | Authors still wire steps | High |
| O2 | P/P/P; dependency order from finite effects | Strong declarativity without general solver | Effect conflicts and final-state checking | High |
| O3 | P/P/P; transition admission and outcome derivation | Legal lifecycle visible | Workflow bias and case coverage | High/very high |
| O4 | P/P/P; derives change topology from desired facts | Least manual wiring | Matching, deletion and planner risk | Very high |

All authority and concurrency claims are unproved until runtime tests. Required cases:
two proposals on one base, accept/discard race, first acceptance, equivalent acceptance,
same content with changed evidence, unauthorized field replacement, replay with changed
intent, cancellation before effects and unknown completion after commit request.
An input list is not authorization, and idempotence is not proof of commit outcome.

Evidence: [verb grammar](../../grammar/garns.lark), [resolver](../../src/garns/resolve.py),
[runtime limitations](../../docs/LIMITATIONS.md), [A4 publication](../../docs/adr/A4-revision-publication.md)
and [A7 retry](../../docs/adr/A7-isolation-retry.md). Implementing any option requires
new consumers and reviewed API/plan amendments, not just a new keyword.
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/ParameterizedModules.md
# Parameterized Module Alternatives

Requirement: type, intent, pure predicate and whole-module parameters; scoped names
such as A::ID and B::ID; selective visibility; no loss of original dependencies or
constraints. Runtime users and expected revisions remain operation inputs.

All options resolve names by identity, not textual substitution. Selection of an
export never prunes its semantic dependency closure. Instantiation has an explicit
identity policy: two named instances are distinct unless one explicitly aliases
the other. Equal arguments do not silently merge their data. Compiler code generation
may share implementation without sharing model identity.

## M1 Checked template specialization

```text
module Versioned<type T, intent Key, predicate Check, module Evidence> { ... }
module Public = Versioned<Subject, catalog::ID, Valid, Observations>
module Private = Versioned<Subject, catalog::ID, Valid, Observations>
```

Expand a generic module for each explicit instance, then resolve the specialized
module. Parameter kinds are checked at the boundary; required member names on a
module parameter are discovered from the template body and checked at instantiation.
No inferred structural compatibility exists beyond those explicit uses.

Valid: Public and Private have separate qualified resource identities while both
reference the supplied catalog::ID intent. Refusal: Evidence lacks Revision, Check
has wrong types, recursive specialization never terminates, or unqualified ID is
ambiguous. Definition-site references remain bound before argument substitution.

Cost medium/high; reuses the existing resolver after hygienic expansion. Errors may
arrive late and repeat per instance. Evolution maps instance identity, arguments and
template definition version. Fits B1/C1; weaker modular reasoning than signatures,
but legitimate and familiar as a design organization without claiming C++ compatibility.

## M2 Explicit module signatures

```text
signature EvidenceModel { export intent ID; export resource Revision }
module Versioned<type T, intent Key, predicate Check(T), module E: EvidenceModel> { ... }
module Public = Versioned<Subject, catalog::ID, Valid, Observations>
```

Check generic bodies against signatures before instantiation. The signature states
required exported declarations, kinds and invariant obligations. Exposing E::Revision
does not flatten E. Selection and renaming bind local aliases to stable identities.
Substitution must not weaken a promised constraint.

Valid: two independently implemented evidence modules satisfy the same contract.
Refusal: Revision exists but has an incompatible identity/reference role, or an
implementation claims a required predicate without enforcing it. Avoid general
logical implication solving: require matching named obligations, explicit witnesses
within a closed proof mechanism, or refuse unproved compatibility.

Cost high, best predictability for large reusable schemas. Fits B2 or B3/C2/C3.
Needs explicit sharing declarations if two parameters must name the same type or
resource; matching names are insufficient. Signature evolution and instance/schema
evolution are separate changes, both visible in the plan.

## M3 Constrained selective inclusion

```text
module Combined<module A: Source, module B: Source> {
  include A select {ID, Entry} as Left
  include B select {ID, Entry} as Right
  export Left::Entry, Right::Entry
}
```

Modules compose through selected exports, contracts and explicit refinements. This
is the closest to scoped inheritance, but no override changes inherited meaning
silently. An override requires an explicit replacement/continuity declaration and
proof that exported obligations still hold. Inclusion order cannot choose winners.

Valid: Left::ID and Right::ID coexist; Left::Entry retains its original Left::ID.
Refusal: both selected as bare ID, diamond inclusion with conflicting refinements,
or hiding a required dependency in a way that drops its constraint. Hidden dependencies
remain in the compiled closure; hiding means not author-visible, not nonexistent.

Cost high/very high; attractive for broad reuse but introduces more interaction rules.
Fits B3/R3/C3. Evolution must say whether including an existing module references its
existing identities or instantiates a fresh copy; this option defaults to references
and requires explicit instantiation for new identities. Namespaces alone are not copies.

## M4 Explicit module ports and linking

```text
module Versioned {
  input type Content
  input intent Key
  input predicate Valid(Content)
  input module Evidence: EvidenceModel
}
assembly Public {
  instance V: Versioned
  bind V.Content = Subject
  bind V.Key = catalog::ID
  bind V.Valid = ValidSubject
  bind V.Evidence = Observations
}
```

Separate reusable definitions from an explicit assembly graph. Bindings are data;
compiler derives dependency order and validates completeness before creating a model.
Port paths are qualified, and selected outputs are separately exposed. No service
discovery or implicit provider selection is involved.

Valid: reuse the same module definition in two named assemblies. Refusal: unbound
port, two bindings to one port, incompatible signature or a forbidden assembly cycle.
Referencing a supplied module retains its identities; a new instance creates new ones.

Cost high; verbose but explicit, strong fit with T4 and R2 named relationships.
Reduces template lookup surprises, adds an assembly layer for small programs. Changes
to bindings have first-class migration impact; an author cannot casually reconnect a
persistent instance to a new identity and call it a build-only change.

## Comparison

| Option | Gates and derivation | Strength | Concern | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | P/P/P; hygienic specialization | Smaller conceptual addition | Late body errors | Medium/high |
| M2 | P/P/P; contract-checked substitution | Early predictable composition | Signature and sharing design | High |
| M3 | P/P/P; closure-preserving inclusion | Selective reuse/refinement | Override and diamond interactions | High/very high |
| M4 | P/P/P; binding graph resolution | Visible assembly and provenance | Wiring verbosity | High |

Projected only. Common mutants: swapped intent/type argument, wrong predicate arity,
two ID imports, captured local name, same-argument identity collapse, hidden dependency
loss and cyclic instantiation. Current named imports are the baseline; no generic
module syntax is implemented. Evidence: [grammar](../../grammar/garns.lark),
[resolver](../../src/garns/resolve.py), [reference](../../docs/DSL_REFERENCE.md).
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/README.md
# GARNS Design Option Index

Status: design exploration, not accepted language syntax or working compiler support.
Every code block in this directory is illustrative unless labelled current GARNS.

| Area | Four alternatives |
| --- | --- |
| [Relationships](Relationships.md) | R1 inline policies; R2 named relationships; R3 relationship contracts; R4 integrity rules |
| [Value constraints](ValueConstraints.md) | C1 intent invariants; C2 refined types; C3 predicate contracts; C4 constraint theories |
| [Intent bundles](IntentBundles.md) | B1 traits; B2 typed expansion bundles; B3 module exports; B4 companion rules |
| [World and targets](WorldAndTargets.md) | T1 separate declarations; T2 world sections; T3 reusable profiles; T4 target applications |
| [Document values](DocumentValues.md) | V1 document scalar; V2 structural values; V3 format contracts; V4 explicit value graph |
| [Parameterized modules](ParameterizedModules.md) | M1 specialization; M2 module signatures; M3 constrained inclusion; M4 explicit linking |
| [Mutation contracts](MutationContracts.md) | O1 bounded compounds; O2 effect contracts; O3 state transitions; O4 desired-state reconciliation |

Read [the combined comparison](../LanguageDesignExploration.md) after the individual
areas. [Baseline](Baseline.md) separates existing behavior from proposed changes.
[Examples](examples/README.md) compares the same model under four combinations.

## Reading the assessments

The three declarativity gates are explicit ordering, inspectable descriptions and
meaningful engine derivation. `P/P/P` means all three have a specified design argument,
not measured compiler conformance. Every option below has that projected gate result
under its stated restrictions. An implementation violating those restrictions would
lose that classification. The entries are intentionally not assigned numerical scores.

Correctness remains unproved for all new designs. Each file names refusal examples
and decision procedures that a future compiler/runtime must implement. The common
operation laws and evaluator provenance are recorded in the combined comparison.

Costs are relative design judgments: low, medium, high or very high compared with
the existing implementation. They are not time estimates. No option is free merely
because its spelling resembles current GARNS.
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/Relationships.md
# Relationship Design Alternatives

Requirement: express reference identity, target deletion, absence and joint
uniqueness without requiring database concepts. Current `end` defaults to restrict;
detach requires optional; all key flags together form one uniqueness rule.
Standalone unenforced is distinct from a deletion action.

The examples use a Decision pointing to Proposal and RevisionEvidence identified
by owner plus role. SQL foreign keys are one adapter strategy, never the definition.

## R1 Inline link policies

```text
link proposal -> Proposal { deletion restrict key }
link result -> Revision { deletion detach optional }
use evidence_role { key }
link owner -> Revision { deletion restrict key }
```

Keep the existing model and improve its vocabulary. Qualified links resolve as
today; the compiler gathers all key participants after expansion. Deletion refers
to the target: cascade removes owners, detach clears references, restrict refuses.
No source order determines behavior. Unenforced references explicitly lack existence
and target-deletion guarantees; combining them with a deletion action refuses.

Refusal: required result plus detach. Valid case: optional result detached when its
target is deleted, provided resulting invariants still hold. A valid but duplicate
owner/role pair refuses even if each field individually appears elsewhere.

IR largely unchanged; adapters enforce the declared action or refuse capability.
Expose a resolved-key explanation so authors see the combined uniqueness rule.
Migration cost low for spelling, medium for end-to-end enforcement. Best with B1/B2;
expansion must not accidentally merge unrelated keys. The implicit joint-key grouping
is compact but surprising for multiple independent uniqueness requirements.

## R2 Named relationship declarations

```text
relationship DecisionProposal {
  owner Decision as proposal
  target Proposal
  cardinality exactly_one
  target_deletion restrict
}
unique Decision by proposal
unique RevisionEvidence by owner, evidence_role
```

Relationships are first-class named facts outside carrier bodies. The endpoint role
introduces a reference-valued member; named uniqueness separates identity constraints
from the reference itself. The compiler builds an adjacency model and generated
accessors; no independent duplicate inline link is permitted.

Refusal: two relationships introduce Decision.proposal without aliases, or detach
on exactly_one. Valid case: a zero_or_one result role with detach. Preserve standalone
unenforced as a relationship mode, not as an action. Unknown targets are admissible
only under that mode; present targets retain the declared logical reference type.

New relationship IR can lower to existing link primitives for simple cases. Named
constraint identities help diagnostics and migration; moving an inline link needs
an explicit continuity map. Cost medium. Fits M4 linking and O4 relation-oriented
effects. Concern: reading one carrier requires following external declarations;
tooling must show the assembled view. More typing is not automatically more declarative.

## R3 Reusable relationship contracts

```text
relation_contract OwnedEvidence<Owner, Target> {
  reference owner: Owner required target_deletion restrict
  reference target: Target required target_deletion restrict
  value evidence_role
  unique owner, evidence_role
}
association RevisionEvidence satisfies OwnedEvidence<Revision, Revision>
```

Define recurring relationship patterns once and instantiate them with types and
roles. Contracts retain invariant and uniqueness identity through substitution.
Applying a contract expands its members and obligations, not a separate nested
entity. Explicit aliases are needed for repeated instances.

Refusal: an implementation weakens required target existence or forgets the joint
uniqueness guarantee. Valid case: two associations reuse the owner/role pattern with
different owner types. A separate optional-reference contract permits detach;
unenforced is a different contract that cannot satisfy enforced existence.

IR records instantiation origin plus expanded links/constraints; adapters consume
the expansion. Migration tracks contract-version effects at every use. Cost high,
especially if added beside another incompatible template system. Best with M2/M3
and B3; avoid inventing a second kind of module parameter. Good reuse for large models,
less useful for one-off links; a bad shared contract has a wide impact.

## R4 Named integrity rules over typed references

```text
link proposal: ref Proposal
rule decision_target: every Decision.proposal resolves
rule one_decision: unique Decision(proposal)
rule evidence_identity: unique RevisionEvidence(owner, evidence_role)
on target_deleted(Revision): detach optional references selected by ResultRole
```

Separate reference shape from a closed set of named integrity rules. This is not
an arbitrary trigger language: admit existence, uniqueness and explicit deletion
actions only. Unenforced omits existence by an explicit exemption; accidental rule
omission is rejected by a requirement that every link states a reference policy.

The compiler normalizes rules to a constraint graph and detects conflicting delete
policies before execution. Refusal: both restrict and cascade apply to the same role;
required references cannot be detached. Valid case: one rule applies a policy to an
explicit named collection of roles. Action order is derived from the dependency
graph; cycles or inconsistent resulting constraints refuse, not first-rule wins.

Cost high. Useful with C4/B4 if one shared rule algebra exists; difficult with implicit
inheritance and open-ended selectors. Evolution needs explicit membership diffs so
a newly selected role cannot silently acquire cascade. Its advantage is model-wide
policy reuse; its risk is hidden reach and harder local explanation.

## Comparison and proof obligations

| Option | Declarativity gates | Strongest benefit | Principal concern | Relative cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R1 | P/P/P: link resolution and joint constraint derivation | Local, familiar, minimal change | Implicit joint-key grouping | Low/medium |
| R2 | P/P/P: endpoint assembly from named declarations | Visible relationship identity | Fragmented authoring | Medium |
| R3 | P/P/P: typed contract expansion | Reusable relationship semantics | Contract/template machinery | High |
| R4 | P/P/P: closed rule graph normalization | Shared model-wide policies | Nonlocal action scope | High |

All are design arguments, not implemented checks. For each, qualify restrict,
cascade, detach, missing required targets, standalone unenforced, joint-key conflicts
and deletion-induced invariant failures. Delete closure and ledger effects must
remain within one governed transaction. Do not select solely by source brevity.

Current evidence: [grammar](../../grammar/garns.lark), [resolver](../../src/garns/resolve.py)
`resolve_link`, and [reference](../../docs/DSL_REFERENCE.md) Links. No known historical
decision requires the word end rather than deletion; its rename is a proposal.
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/ValueConstraints.md
# Reusable Value Constraint Alternatives

Requirement: positivity belongs with the meaning of a revision number; a shared
lower/upper relationship travels with its bundle. Resource-specific laws remain
at the resource. Existing pattern/length metadata must not remain unenforced.

All options distinguish value validity from operation preconditions. A value rule
cannot inspect clock, caller identity or arbitrary mutable state. Cross-reference
rules require an affected-object dependency plan or explicit capability refusal.

## C1 Invariants directly on intents

```text
intent revision_number: Integer { invariant value > 0 }
trait Bounds { use lower use upper invariant lower <= upper }
```

Add one value binding to intent predicates. Every use inherits the conjunction of
the intent's invariants; the carrier gets substituted predicates with origin data.
Bundled predicates bind companion names at declaration time. Type meaning remains
the current base type plus intent identity, not a new refinement type universe.

Valid: revision_number used by two unrelated carriers, both rejecting zero. Refusal:
a default of zero, or a predicate referring to another carrier's undeclared field.
Optional absence skips the value predicate; explicit nullable values need a declared
null policy. Presence laws are separate, so skipping is not accidental permission.

Cost medium: grammar/IR additions are small, enforcement across writes and migration
is not. Fits B1/R1 and incremental evolution. Constraint strengthening produces a
validation/migration obligation; it cannot silently invalidate stored values.
Weakness: independently named intents cannot conveniently share the same refinement
without another predicate mechanism. Diagnostics can still name original intent/use.

## C2 Reusable refined value types

```text
type PositiveRevision = Integer where self > 0
intent revision_number: PositiveRevision
bundle Bounds { lower: Integer upper: Integer invariant lower <= upper }
```

Separate reusable value validity from the domain role supplied by an intent. Types
may refine types by conjunction; cycles refuse. Bundle predicates remain relational
because neither bound is independently valid or invalid without its companion.
Do not infer arbitrary theorem-based subtyping: exact type identity or an explicit
checked conversion establishes compatibility.

Valid: two differently named intents share PositiveRevision without sharing their
identities. Refusal: assigning an unchecked Integer to PositiveRevision, even if the
author assumes positivity; a checked construction may accept it after validation.
Defaults and transformed migration values pass the same constructor gate.

IR carries named refinement closures; generated APIs share validators. Cost medium/high;
existing newtypes need a clear relationship to refinement aliases and nominal identity.
Fits M2 typed parameters and V2 structural values. Potential boilerplate for one-use
types is offset by predictable reuse. Predicate language stays bounded and decidable
to type-check, without promising satisfiability proofs for every formula.

## C3 Parameterized predicate contracts

```text
predicate Positive(x: Integer) = x > 0
predicate Ordered(lo: Integer, hi: Integer) = lo <= hi
intent revision_number: Integer requires Positive(value)
bundle Bounds requires Ordered(lower, upper)
```

Predicates are named, pure, typed declarations. Intents, bundles and module parameters
refer to them; the compiler substitutes qualified arguments and computes obligation
dependencies. This prioritizes sharing laws, rather than introducing many value types.
Constraint invocation is data, not a callback into Python.

Valid: one module takes a predicate parameter with a declared signature. Refusal:
a predicate with wrong argument types, recursion outside the admitted algebra, or an
authority/clock lookup masquerading as value validity. Optional guards are explicit;
each predicate declares admissible arguments instead of implicitly swallowing null.

Cost medium/high; must avoid duplicating C2 validators behind different syntax.
Fits B3/M3 contracts; C1 can lower into it but should not create two meanings for the
same condition. Schema evolution records the predicate definition digest and affected
uses, not merely its unchanged name. Local readability suffers if trivial rules are
always indirect; permit inlining for explanations, not semantic copying.

## C4 Closed constraint theories

```text
theory RevisionRules {
  forall r in Revision: r.revision_number > 0
  forall b in carries(Bounds): b.lower <= b.upper
}
world Catalog uses RevisionRules
```

Declare integrity over typed sets; compiler derives each rule's affected objects and
validation plan. Restrict quantification to finite, declared domains and a typed,
side-effect-free algebra. This can express uniqueness and cross-object conditions
without restating them per carrier. No unbounded solver is implied.

Valid: an update to a referenced bound triggers validation of dependent owners.
Refusal: unbounded domain selection, unsupported aggregate semantics, conflicting
theory imports or an adapter unable to protect the read/write dependency set.
No source-order override is permitted; compatible rules conjoin.

Cost very high: global dependency tracking, bounded evaluation and concurrency
protection. Fits R4/B4; mixed with C1 requires one normalized constraint identity.
Migration is model-wide theory validation. Strongest expressive reach, weakest
locality; broad expressiveness may increase runtime refusal rather than useful reuse.
Proof obligations must separate satisfiability, type checking and runtime preservation.

## Shared validation boundary

All options must validate inputs/defaults and the operation's final affected state.
Intermediate private values may be staged inside a declared atomic effect graph;
they cannot escape or be read as accepted state. Current per-mutation validation is
not silently changed: transaction-final checks are an explicit runtime amendment.
Whether transient states are admitted must be fixed before a backend is qualified.

| Option | Gates and useful derivation | Concern | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C1 | P/P/P; propagates intent predicates | Shared rules across distinct intents | Medium |
| C2 | P/P/P; derives refinement closure | Nominal versus refined compatibility | Medium/high |
| C3 | P/P/P; binds and specializes predicate contracts | Indirection and template interactions | Medium/high |
| C4 | P/P/P; derives affected-state validation | Boundedness and concurrency complexity | Very high |

Every correctness claim is projected. Mutants: zero default, missing predicate
dependency, changed target violating an owner, null passed to non-null predicate,
and a strengthening migration with invalid legacy data. No passing runtime is claimed.
See [baseline](Baseline.md), [engine](../../src/garns/engine.py) and
[invariant resolver](../../src/garns/resolve_read.py).
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/language-design-options/WorldAndTargets.md
# World and Target Alternatives

Requirement: authors can see the semantic world, generated languages/API styles and
persistence destinations without confusing them. Choosing PostgreSQL is not declaring
a different meaning from SQLite. API async behavior is distinct from adapter strategy
and from SQLite's durability setting of a similar name.

Each option preserves a semantic-world digest separately from build/deployment
identity. Durability, writer authority and isolation remain promises, not optional
tuning. Multiple targets mean separate deployments unless explicitly designed otherwise;
none of these options provides cross-store transactions or replication.

## T1 Separate world, build and deployment declarations

```text
world Catalog { modules catalog durability durable writers governed scope deployment }
build PythonApi for Catalog { language python style async }
deployment Local for Catalog { backend sqlite location env CATALOG_DB }
deployment Server for Catalog { backend postgres location env CATALOG_DB }
```

Move generated targets out of world; retain existing deployment responsibility.
Compiler resolves references and verifies each selected target's capabilities against
the world. Selecting a build/deployment is explicit; there is no last-definition wins.

Valid: same world with two alternative deployments. Refusal: a deployment cannot
provide required atomicity, or two artifacts target the same output name. Future
Python sync and async builds are separately named, not ambiguous variants.

Cost low/medium, mainly IR/configuration separation and artifact identity. Fits any
module model. Concern: world-facing discovery requires an assembled summary across
declarations; provide a resolved-target view. Migration can largely extract existing
generated entries mechanically. No need to put runtime secrets in source.

## T2 Explicit sections associated with a world

```text
world Catalog {
  model { modules catalog durability durable writers governed scope deployment }
  generate { PythonApi: python async }
  persist { Local: sqlite at env CATALOG_DB; Server: postgres at env CATALOG_DB }
}
```

One author-facing composition, three typed sections. The model section alone defines
semantic-world identity; nested configurations have separate identities and validation.
Persistence entries are named alternatives, with explicit selection for execution.

Valid: inspect one declaration to find all targets. Refusal: backend tuning in model,
an ambiguous default deployment, or duplicate artifact names. A reusable module
cannot secretly select an application's destination through import side effects.

Cost medium, straightforward normalization to the same separate IR records as T1.
Best fit to the operator's desire for world-visible targets. Larger applications
may need referenced configuration fragments to avoid giant files; those fragments
must have explicit merge/conflict rules, never ambient inheritance. Migration maps
old world/deployment sources into sections without changing promises.

## T3 Named reusable target profiles

```text
profile AsyncPython { api python async }
profile DurableLocal { persistence sqlite; requires durable }
world Catalog { modules catalog guarantees DurableGoverned }
assembly LocalCatalog { world Catalog; apply AsyncPython, DurableLocal }
```

Profiles are typed parameterized target bundles, not unrestricted configuration maps.
The assembly derives all artifacts after resolving explicit profile parameters.
Conflicting single-valued settings refuse; there is no priority based on file order.
Multiple named output variants remain possible.

Valid: several worlds reuse the same API packaging profile. Refusal: incompatible
profiles choose two engines for one named deployment, or weaken the world's guarantee.
Defaults must appear in the resolved plan and be pinned by profile version.

Cost medium/high, benefits a larger deployment estate. Fits M2/M3 profile signatures;
introduces indirection for one small application. Evolution needs profile-diff impact
across assemblies, not just a schema migration. Does not authorize implicit physical
names: bindings remain explicit inputs or separately reviewed generated artifacts.

## T4 Explicit target applications

```text
world Catalog = CatalogModel<Content>
artifact PythonApi = PythonAsync<Catalog>
store Local = SQLite<Catalog, LocalBinding>
store Server = PostgreSQL<Catalog, ServerBinding>
```

Treat code generation and persistence as typed applications of target constructors
to a semantic world. Constructors declare capabilities and outputs; they are not
database modules inheriting into the domain. Dependency analysis derives build order
and invalidation. Distinct applications have explicit identities.

Valid: one world supplies several generated projections with separate bindings.
Refusal: wrong binding world/version, missing guarantee, or circular artifact dependency.
No declaration executes a connection open merely by being resolved.

Cost high, unifies with M4 explicit linking but requires a typed artifact graph.
Useful if multiple output families become central. Risks turning a data language into
a general build system; restrict constructors to declared compiler/adapter kinds.
Migration records semantic origin separately from artifact dependency changes.

## Comparison

| Option | Gates and derivation | Best property | Concern | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| T1 | P/P/P; world-to-target validation | Smallest migration | Targets dispersed | Low/medium |
| T2 | P/P/P; section normalization and validation | Clear one-place view | Large configurations need references | Medium |
| T3 | P/P/P; typed profile composition | Reuse across deployments | Defaults and override surprise | Medium/high |
| T4 | P/P/P; artifact dependency derivation | Uniform target application | Build-language scope creep | High |

No target is implemented by these examples. All candidates retain async-only current
API scope and accepted adapter obligations. Capability admission, target conflict,
semantic digest stability and generation-path collision are the common proof cases.
Evidence: [world/deployment grammar](../../grammar/garns.lark),
[direction](../../docs/GARNS_DIRECTION.md), [accepted scope](../W1-ACCEPTANCE.md).
++++++++++--------:8d8f2250-2a19-47f0-910f-098ffcbee120:t:garns-wz/garns/dev-docs/LanguageChangeRegister.md
# GARNS language change register

Status: discussion proposals collected during the Pyrocat modeling exercise.
No grammar, runtime or accepted specification has changed. This is a growing list,
not an implementation approval or a replacement for the v9-6 scope decisions.

The proposed comparison process is in
[LanguageDesignExplorationProposal.md](LanguageDesignExplorationProposal.md).
The operator authorized that exploration. Its completed design comparison is in
[LanguageDesignExploration.md](LanguageDesignExploration.md), with seven area
documents and four combined example sets. The alternatives remain proposals;
no language choice, grammar change or implementation has been accepted.

## Purpose

Improve how authors declare data meaning, reference behavior and reusable value
constraints. The syntax should communicate semantics without requiring knowledge
of tables, columns or a particular database's constraint mechanisms.

## L1: Replace the link keyword end with deletion

Current syntax:

```garns
link owner -> Revision { end restrict key }
```

Proposed spelling, endorsed for this change list:

```garns
link owner -> Revision { deletion restrict key }
link base -> Revision { deletion restrict optional }
```

The deletion policy applies when the referenced target is deleted. Preserve the
three existing behaviors: restrict prevents target deletion while referenced;
cascade deletes the referencing object; detach removes the reference and therefore
requires an optional link. Key and optional remain independent modifiers.

Reason: end suggests process completion and does not identify deletion. The new
word makes the event explicit. This is a naming change, not a storage feature.

Before implementation: decide old-spelling compatibility and deprecation, then
update grammar, diagnostics, documentation and canonical fixtures together. Do not
silently reassign attributes during source migration. Retain standalone unenforced
semantics and its conflict with deletion actions.

## L2: Invariants on scalar intents

Requirement: a rule inherent to a value's meaning is declared with its intent and
inherited by every use. A revision number must be positive wherever it appears.
Resource invariants remain for relationships specific to an owning object.

Illustrative syntax only; value binding name is undecided:

```garns
intent revision_number : Integer "positive acceptance number" {
  invariant value > 0
}
```

Current language admits resource/member invariants, not this intent item. Existing
text pattern/length refinements and closed sets need one coherent validation model;
pattern and length currently lack runtime consumers. Do not add more declarations
that merely parse without enforcing their meaning.

Decisions: binding the current value; multiple predicates (propose conjunction);
composition with use-level rules; whether absence skips a value predicate for an
optional use; diagnostics naming the intent and failing use. Validation applies to
defaults, repairs, imports, mutations and migrations, not only generated setters.
Read/backend violations must report contract failure rather than silently coercing.

## L3: Macro-like intent bundles and shared invariants

Requirement, clarified by the operator: using one intent bundle introduces its
companion intents wherever it is used. Declare the companions and their shared
rules once; every use inherits them. This is semantic expansion into the owning
carrier, not a nested structured value, collection or separately identified object.
A range bundle could introduce lower and upper intents plus lower <= upper.

Existing trait/carry composition already expands uses and links. Assess whether
extending or clarifying that mechanism satisfies this requirement before adding
a competing construct. Expansion must propagate invariants as well as fields.
Macro-like means typed, qualified expansion, not arbitrary text substitution.

Decisions: bundle declaration and use spelling; companion naming and repeated use;
transitive expansion and cycle refusal; collision and override rules; inherited
key/default/optional policies; cross-companion invariant binding; diagnostics that
retain declaration and use locations; evolution when a shared bundle changes.
Presence rules and validation timing must be explicit. No new syntax is fixed yet.

Adapters choose the representation of the expanded meaning. Additional columns
are one possible relational mapping, not the definition of the feature.

## L4: Collection-valued intents and collection invariants

Separate, unapproved extension, not the operator's meaning of L3: a sequence or set of values, with
element rules and rules over the collection? Examples: every score is nonnegative;
a collection is nonempty; values are unique; an ordered sequence is monotonic.

Existing list_of support is restricted and does not admit collection shapes on
intents; nested constructors are also restricted. Inspect and deliberately amend
those rules rather than treating this as an already available extension.

Decisions: sequence versus set semantics, order/duplicate handling, element versus
whole-value predicates, quantification, absence versus empty, nested structured
elements, finite bounds and diagnostics identifying the failing element. Keep this
distinct from L3 until the intended scope is agreed.

## L5: Separate world semantics from build and deployment settings

Requirement: distinguish what a data system means and guarantees from how its
artifacts are generated and where it runs. The current world body mixes module
composition, durability, writer and scope contracts with generated target choices.
It should not read as an undifferentiated collection of compiler switches.

Proposed responsibility split, with syntax and exact placement still undecided:

- Semantic world: included modules, relationships across those modules, scope and
  isolation, and required durability and governed-write guarantees.
- Build/generation configuration: requested output languages and artifacts, such
  as the current generated python/rust selection.
- Deployment and adapter configuration: backend, location, pool and operational
  settings that realize the declared world contract.

Operator clarification: the world-facing configuration must also expose persistence
destinations, such as SQLite or PostgreSQL, alongside generated language targets
such as Python or Rust. These are distinct target categories, not one generated
list. Separating responsibilities need not require separate top-level declarations:
explicit sections or references associated with a world are a design option.
The current grammar already selects engine in a deployment; decide how that
selection is presented through the world without introducing conflicting sources
of truth. Listing a backend is not a claim that its adapter is implemented.
Whether a world can select multiple persistence targets for separate deployments
is distinct from writing to several stores at once; this proposal does not imply
replication, federated queries or cross-database transactions.

Operator clarification: target selection should leave room for multiple generated
API variants from the same model, for example Python synchronous and Python
asynchronous surfaces together. Keep language, API execution style and persistence
backend distinct dimensions. Adapter execution strategy is a further concern:
an asynchronous public API can use a synchronous SQLite driver behind a worker;
SQLite's storage semantics do not become a different data model. Avoid confusing
API execution style with SQLite's similarly named durability setting.

Current scope remains asynchronous public database APIs only. Synchronous API
generation is future design capacity, not an implementation or release obligation.
Future variants must preserve the same data and transaction semantics, have
unambiguous artifact names, and declare supported target combinations explicitly.
Unsupported combinations must refuse rather than silently substitute a blocking
implementation. A synchronous surface is not assumed to be a trivial wrapper
around the async API; ownership and event-loop interoperability require design.

Durability and writer rules affect observable behavior; do not silently turn them
into optional backend tuning. Scope deployment currently means deployment-wide
scope rather than a row-level scope path; preserve that meaning while reviewing
its wording. Audit quarantine retention and the remaining world items individually
instead of assuming that every operational-sounding declaration is a build option.

Decisions: separate declarations/files versus explicit sections; references from
build and deployment configurations to a world; compatibility with existing world
sources; which changes alter semantic identity versus generated artifacts or
deployment admission. Adapters must satisfy the world contract or refuse it, not
weaken it to match backend capabilities. No grammar change is approved here.

## L6: Distinguish missing from explicit null

Requirement: document values preserve an absent member separately from a member
whose value is null. The current optional/present/absent model, lowered to SQL NULL
by the SQLite implementation, does not provide this three-state document contract.
A Python missing sentinel is a target representation, not the language semantics.
Specify omission, explicit null and a present non-null value independently of
storage. Partial-update omission can mean unchanged; removing a member must be a
separate explicit effect. Decide allowed nullability and presence per value shape,
including defaults, predicates, serialization and migration behavior.

## L7: Parameterized modules and qualified composition

Requirement: reusable modules declare compile-time parameters for types, intents,
predicates and whole modules. An intent argument retains its qualified identity,
value type and constraints; it is not reduced to its underlying scalar. Module
arguments satisfy declared contracts and expose members through a parameter
namespace. Runtime inputs remain operation parameters.

The operator proposes template-style notation and selective composition of
parameterized modules. Consider explicit qualification such as A::ID and B::ID;
these spellings are illustrative and are not accepted by the current grammar.
The resolver already preserves module-qualified identities and named import
aliases. Build on those semantics rather than textual substitution.

Requirements for the design:

- Define parameter kinds, required contracts, substitution and instantiation
  identity. Distinct named instances must not accidentally share data identities.
- Select or alias names entering local scope; refuse ambiguous unqualified names.
- Preserve declaration-site bindings inside imported resources and predicates.
- Keep dependencies and constraints even when their names are not exposed locally.
- Specify nested instantiation, cycle refusal, diagnostics and schema evolution.
- Keep predicate parameters typed and within an admitted pure expression algebra,
  rather than arbitrary host-language callbacks.

Inheritance versus composition terminology and exact syntax remain open. These
features are proposed, not a claim that current modules accept parameters.

## L8: Declarative mutation operations

Requirement: modules declare named mutation operations alongside read-only query
and question declarations. Each operation distinguishes inputs, authority,
preconditions, effects, returns and its atomic boundary. Queries remain read-only.

Existing restricted, bulk, alias and compound declarations are resolved and
validated but have no executing or generated verb surface in the current baseline.
Extend or consolidate that model; do not introduce a parallel mutation system or
claim that adding declarations to catalog.garns alone implements this feature.

Semantic example: accepting a proposal requires authority for its document, a
pending proposal, an expected base that is still current, and validation matching
the exact candidate and pinned evidence. A changed acceptance creates a revision
and its evidence plus one accepted decision atomically. Equivalent acceptance
records a decision pointing to the existing revision. A competing acceptance or
discard cannot satisfy incompatible terminal outcomes. Returns identify the
outcome; they are distinct from the persisted effects.

Decisions and proof obligations:

- Typed inputs and result shapes, with explicit absence and null semantics.
- Operation authority and object scope, plus permitted field changes. Identity
  providers remain outside GARNS; a field input allowlist alone is not authority.
- Expected-state checks and effects enforced as one atomic operation, including
  concurrency protection rather than a separate application-side preflight.
- Preconditions versus resulting-state invariants, including when shared and
  cross-object invariants are checked and which dependent objects are affected.
- Allowed transitions and immutable/append-only data enforced through every
  admitted write route, not only a generated convenience method.
- Failure, retry identity, equivalent outcomes and unknown completion consistent
  with accepted GARNS transaction contracts; no independent retry mechanism.
- Backend-neutral effects and adapter capability refusal before side effects.

This is a language and runtime design revision, not only a naming amendment.

## Scope of the combined revision

The collected proposals now span reusable declarations, value semantics, operation
contracts and target configuration. Prepare a coherent design with clearly staged
implementation rather than treating them as one small grammar patch. Separate
existing-but-unconsumed semantics from genuinely new language features. Preserve
accepted runtime contracts unless an explicit reviewed amendment changes them.

## Naming observations without proposed semantic changes

- link is a reference-valued field; its name is the field name and its target is
  the referenced type. It need not be a physical foreign-key column in every target.
- All key-marked uses and links form one combined uniqueness rule, not independent
  uniqueness constraints. Keep this meaning explicit in author documentation.
- optional permits absence of the reference; it does not disable target validation
  when the reference is present.
- association declares a relationship with its own values and constraints. A table
  is one adapter representation. No rename for link/key/association is agreed.

## Design and proof sequence

1. Review names and value semantics before fixing grammar or implementation syntax.
2. Map each proposal onto qualified IR, validation, generated surfaces, queries,
   evolution and adapter obligations; refuse unsupported combinations explicitly.
3. Prove inherited intent constraints with canonical valid/invalid fixtures,
   including multiple uses and defaults. Prove bundle expansion preserves shared
   constraints. Collection changes require a separate scope decision.
4. Prove behavior through claimed target adapters without changing domain meaning.

Sources: docs/DSL_REFERENCE.md (Types, Intents, Carriers and Invariants),
docs/LIMITATIONS.md, docs/GARNS_DIRECTION.md (Language policy), and
dev-docs/GarnsV9-6-PostgresAsyncImplementationPlan.md (scope). These proposals
need an explicit language-scope decision because the current backend iteration
preserves the ratified grammar.
